WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.220
The strongest character in "Encanto" is middle
sister Luisa — but if this movie had been

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.550
made in the ‘90s, that probably wouldn't
have been the case.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:11.269
The reason why is something only adults will
notice.

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:16.250
When you think about Disney, you usually think
of princesses -- especially when it comes

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:17.750
to Disney's animated films.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:23.160
But unlike the title characters in recent
movies "Raya and the Last Dragon," "Moana,"

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.710
and "Frozen," Mirabel isn't even technically
a princess, and the Madrigal family isn't

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:28.740
royalty.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.130
That fact is obvious to anyone.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:34.620
But what kids may miss is that even though
they don't live in a castle or sit on thrones,

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.160
the magic granted to the Madrigals gives them
an exalted status in their community.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:43.600
So while they may not officially be considered
royalty, by acting as their community's leaders

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.670
and protectors, the Madrigals are the closest
thing to it.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:51.620
In fact, the Madrigals check many of the boxes
for royal status, including a birthright to

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:56.250
power that's passed down through the generations
and an unspoken understanding that they should

00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:00.070
put the needs of the family ahead of their
own personal desires.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:04.340
Mirabel's lack of a gift excludes her from
some of these requirements, but she grapples

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:08.049
with her own disappointments over being the
only non-magical Madrigal.

00:01:08.049 --> 00:01:13.420
"I call it the 'Not Special Special,' since
you have no gift."

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:15.119
"Uh, thanks?"

00:01:15.119 --> 00:01:19.540
Ultimately, though, Mirabel's determination
to help those she loves is what makes her

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:23.790
most like Disney's other modern, nontraditional
princesses.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:28.030
Given all this, it wouldn't be a surprise
to see Mirabel join Disney's princess lineup

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:31.710
sometime soon, regardless of whether or not
she's officially royalty.

00:01:32.829 --> 00:01:37.790
"Sleeping Beauty" has Maleficent, "The Little
Mermaid" has Ursula, and "Frozen" has Hans.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:42.430
Ever since Disney's first animated feature
"Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs" was released

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:47.490
in 1937, almost all Disney animated films
have had a noteworthy antagonist.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:52.250
Many of those antagonists have become as revered
as their movies' main characters, but "Encanto"

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:54.360
won't be adding to the Disney villain ranks.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:59.100
There is no character in "Encanto" who could
be considered a typical bad guy.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:04.900
Sure, Mirabel is often at odds with her seemingly
perfect sister Isabela, and Abuela Alma discounts

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:09.550
and dismisses Mirabel due to her status as
the only Madrigal without a gift, but these

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:13.810
are just family squabbles between people who
love each other and want to get along.

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:19.160
The real antagonist of "Encanto" is less obvious:
It's the fear that keeps the Madrigals from

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:23.470
being honest with one another, and the pressure
to fulfill their obligations, that threatens

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:25.520
the magic powering their home.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:29.800
So while kids may not notice the movie lacks
a traditional bad guy, many will still be

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:34.480
able to relate to the way their role within
their families may limit what parts of themselves

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:37.300
they can show, even to the people they love
the most.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:43.100
Mirabel's middle sister Luisa was given the
gift of strength, and ever since, she's used

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:47.120
her powers to help the members of the community
with all their heavy lifting.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.980
Whether it's single-handedly rounding up an
escaped herd of donkeys and carrying them

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:55.920
back to their enclosure or moving the local
church to a more optimal location, Luisa is

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.260
constantly busy lifting, hauling, and fixing.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:03.480
For children growing up now, the fact that
Luisa is a woman may seem perfectly natural.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.970
But grown-ups know that if "Encanto" had been
made by Disney a few decades ago, there's

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:12.730
no way this story's physically strongest character
would have been developed as a woman.

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:17.120
The idea might never have even occurred to
the filmmakers -- or if it had, Luisa likely

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:20.170
would have been ridiculed and ostracized for
her strength.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:24.900
Thankfully today, not only can Luisa be a
woman, her strength is taken seriously, and

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:26.790
accepted as the gift it is.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:32.540
Countless people wear glasses, just, not in
movies, and that's particularly true when

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:35.010
it comes to Disney's animated movies.

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:40.220
While glasses-wearing kids will appreciate
seeing Mirabel sport eyewear onscreen in "Encanto,"

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:44.870
what they may not know is just how rare it
is to see a main character with glasses leading

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:46.450
a Disney film.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:50.510
Characters with glasses haven't been part
of the company's features since the beginning,

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:54.770
but it's rare that a main character of a Disney
movie has worn them, making Mirabel one of

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:58.950
the only main characters with glasses in the
Disney animated canon.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:02.320
That Mirabel is a young woman makes her glasses-wearing
even more unique.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:07.060
In fact, if she were to join the Disney princess
lineup, she'd be the only one with glasses.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:11.930
So while Mirabel's glasses are unusual for
a movie character like her, they're also part

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:14.960
of what makes her special in a family full
of special people.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.290
"Gift or no gift, I am just as special as
the rest of my family."

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:24.400
"Frozen" is one of the most successful Disney
animated films in recent memory, and between

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:29.040
the winning performances, touching story,
and memorable songs, it's become a cultural

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:30.040
touchstone.

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:34.440
Consequently, adults may find themselves automatically
comparing the Disney movies that have come

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:39.460
after "Frozen" to the earlier film, especially
"Encanto," which has multiple moments with

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:40.780
obvious parallels.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:45.840
First, when Mirabel attempts to patch things
up with Isabela in an effort to save the family's

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:51.030
magic, she makes her sister so angry that
Isabela loses her tight grip on her flower-generating

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:53.820
powers and creates a prickly cactus.

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:58.310
Isabela is so excited by the realization that
she can use her gift in less-than-perfect

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:03.620
ways that she sings "What Else Can I Do?"
one of the movie's most inspiring songs.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:08.650
Later, when Abuela Alma finds Mirabel after
the Madrigal home collapses, the pair have

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:13.600
a revealing conversation that enables Abuela
to finally admit that she needs to loosen

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:16.790
her grip on her family so all of their lives
can improve.

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:21.720
The way the moment plays out, including Abuela
finding Mirabel and their reconciliation by

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:27.100
a body of water, is reminiscent of the climax
of "Frozen" where Elsa realizes love is the

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:28.880
answer to all of her problems.

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:33.710
Perhaps the most famous name associated with
"Encanto" is that of someone whose voice is

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:38.780
never heard onscreen: Lin-Manuel Miranda,
who not only composed the movie's songs but

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:40.610
helped develop its story.

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:44.560
Adults will recognize Miranda as the creator
of the Broadway hit "Hamilton," and while

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:48.900
some lucky kids may have seen the musical
in person or caught the recorded version on

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:53.460
Disney+, they may not know they've already
heard Miranda's work in another Disney animated

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:54.660
film, "Moana."

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:59.860
Yet, while "Moana" included showstoppers like
"How Far I'll Go" that uphold the Disney film

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:05.420
tradition of accessible, moving songs, adults
will notice Miranda uses the opportunity provided

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:09.860
by "Encanto" to take things in a different
direction with several of its tunes.

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:14.460
In particular, Miranda employs a style more
like the one he used in "Hamilton" in numbers

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:19.360
like "The Family Madrigal" and "We Don't Talk
About Bruno," which include fast, rhythmic

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:24.130
lyrics that unpack a lot of information in
a short amount of time as well as overlapping

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:26.620
melodies that blend and harmonize.

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:31.180
This approach makes the songs in "Encanto"
a bit more theatrical and less cinematic,

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:35.690
but Miranda's evocative lyrics and catchy
song structure ensure the soundtrack adds

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:37.690
joy and color to the film.

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:43.510
"Encanto" reveals that the Madrigal family
has a ritual for receiving their magical gifts.

00:06:43.510 --> 00:06:48.490
On the fifth birthday of each Madrigal child,
the whole town gathers in the family's courtyard

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:54.110
to watch as the child approaches a door that
has been newly created by the house for them.

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:58.919
The child recites some words in front of the
candle that's the source of the Madrigal magic

00:06:58.919 --> 00:07:02.170
and then is imbued with a gift that soon reveals
itself.

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:07.740
They then open their door, unveiling a new
personalized room just for them that's based

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:09.139
on the gift they received.

00:07:09.139 --> 00:07:14.520
This is demonstrated in the movie when Mirabel's
cousin Antonio is granted his gift: the ability

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:16.080
to talk to animals.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:20.800
Immediately after Antonio receives his gift,
he's approached by a toucan who asks Antonio

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:23.400
a question that only he can understand.

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:28.450
When Antonio agrees to the toucan's proposal,
a gang of animals large and small enter the

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:32.900
house and run into Antonio's new room, which
is a magical animal paradise.

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:37.930
However, the animals that populate Antonio's
room and now accompany him everywhere aren't

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:41.430
just any animals — they're animals that
are native to Colombia.

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:48.419
In addition to the toucans, they include raccoon-like
coati, capybaras, the world's largest rodents,

00:07:48.419 --> 00:07:50.680
macaws, tapirs, and a jaguar.

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:55.230
In fact, the production even made sure the
tapirs are specifically mountain tapirs to

00:07:55.230 --> 00:07:59.070
match the mountainous terrain of the area
the Madrigals call home.

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:04.930
As the family member imbued with physical
strength, Luisa is perhaps the most put-upon

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:05.930
Madrigal.

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:09.710
While she never complains, her services seem
to be in constant demand by the people who

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:11.139
live in the Encanto.

00:08:11.139 --> 00:08:15.870
And when Mirabel realizes the constant work
may be getting to her sister, Luisa admits

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.639
to the stress she's feeling in the song "Surface/Pressure."

00:08:18.639 --> 00:08:23.340
In the course of the song she references things
that are likely to go over kids' heads.

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:27.330
Not only does she compare herself to a ship
trying to avoid an iceberg, a clear reference

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:31.320
to the sinking of the Titanic, there are also
two references to Greek mythology.

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:36.289
First, Luisa wonders if Hercules ever felt
the same weight of expectation she feels when

00:08:36.289 --> 00:08:41.410
he went up against Cerberus, the three-headed
dog that guards the Underworld in Greek mythology.

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:45.080
As she sings, the image shows Luisa taking
on Cerberus herself.

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:49.960
What she is referring to, however, is the
twelfth labor of Hercules in which the demigod

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:52.330
fought and subdued the mighty beast.

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:56.541
In addition, during the song, there's an image
of Luisa holding up the globe, a reference

00:08:56.541 --> 00:09:02.210
to the Titan Atlas who, after failing to win
a war against the Olympian gods, was condemned

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:03.970
to hold up the heavens for eternity.

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:09.900
If there's one thing "Encanto" makes abundantly
clear, it's that the Madrigals don't talk

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:13.880
about Bruno, Mirabel's uncle who supposedly
left the family.

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:18.250
However, in the course of the film, Mirabel
discovers that Bruno hasn't gone as far as

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:19.350
everyone thought.

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:23.540
For years he's secretly been living between
the walls of the Madrigals' house with only

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:25.130
rats to talk to.

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:29.620
Needless to say his circumstances have made
him a little eccentric, and part of that eccentricity

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:32.600
is shown in the actions he takes to ward off
bad luck.

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:37.620
When Mirabel first encounters Bruno, he knocks
on the wood all around him, a familiar gesture

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:40.620
to bring good luck that even kids will probably
recognize.

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:45.060
However, Bruno quickly follows that up by
tossing a pinch of salt over his left shoulder,

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:49.950
a gesture he repeats later in the story after
agreeing to use his gift to help Mirabel see

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:51.020
her future.

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:55.760
And while kids may have seen this done before,
they may not realize what adults likely will:

00:09:55.760 --> 00:10:00.270
that throwing salt over one's shoulder is
an obscure bid to drive away bad luck.

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:05.110
Bruno's reliance on these superstitious gestures
speaks to just how nervous and paranoid he's

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:07.690
become during his isolation from his family.

00:10:08.690 --> 00:10:13.620
While the story told in "Encanto" is universal,
the filmmakers have also included many elements

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:17.110
that are specific to Colombia, the country
where it takes place.

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:21.150
One of the most obvious ways they achieve
this is through the use of yellow butterflies,

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:25.540
a motif that's repeated throughout the movie
and is a reference to the work of revered

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:28.300
Colombian writer Gabriel García Márquez.

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:33.360
In particular, yellow butterflies appear in
Márquez's beloved novel "One Hundred Years

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:38.020
of Solitude," where they symbolize love and
conflict and the way the two can come together,

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:40.680
a theme that's important to "Encanto" as well.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:45.760
The filmmakers also took care to ensure the
architecture, costumes, landscape, dance,

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:49.580
and music incorporated into the film are accurate
to Colombia.

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:53.510
This can be seen in the traditional costumes
worn by the characters and the texture of

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:56.790
the materials that were created to design
the Madrigals' house.

00:10:56.790 --> 00:11:02.149
Moreover, songwriter Lin-Manuel Miranda said
he immersed himself in the music of Columbia

00:11:02.149 --> 00:11:04.839
so he could create songs that felt authentic
to the setting.

00:11:04.839 --> 00:11:09.600
He did this by, for example, incorporating
the accordion into the film's opening number

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:12.540
because of the instrument's importance to
Colombian music.

00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:17.340
In fact, the accordion is so essential to
the music of Colombia, Mirabel is seen playing

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:18.540
it during the song.

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:20.870
